## 11. COMPLETION GATES — STRICT DEFINITION OF DONE

The project security remediation process must not be declared complete merely because the audit is finished.

Completion requires all of the following:

### Gate A — Coverage

Every security domain in the required matrix has an evidence-based applicability classification.

No applicable domain has been silently skipped.

### Gate B — Findings

All confirmed critical and high-severity findings have been remediated and verified, or are explicitly documented as blocked by an external dependency or authorization requirement.

No unresolved critical or high issue may be represented as fixed.

### Gate C — Implementation

Every completed remediation has actual corresponding code, configuration, or infrastructure change where a change is required.

A recommendation or report is not implementation.

### Gate D — Testing

Relevant security tests have been executed and their results recorded.

Pre-existing failures, regressions, unexecuted tests, and test limitations are explicitly distinguished.

### Gate E — Regression

No known unresolved regression introduced by the remediation remains unaddressed.

### Gate F — Evidence

Every completed finding has:

- Actual source or configuration evidence.
- Relevant implementation evidence.
- Relevant verification evidence.
- Recorded residual risks, if any.

### Gate G — Persistent State

The state ledger is valid, current, and reconciled with the actual repository.

### Gate H — Remaining Risks

All remaining risks, limitations, blockers, and unverified areas are explicitly recorded.

Never use the phrase "fully secure" or claim that all possible vulnerabilities have been eliminated.

Use `REMEDIATION_COMPLETE_WITH_RECORDED_RESIDUAL_RISKS` only when all completion gates are satisfied.

Use `REMEDIATION_INCOMPLETE` whenever any applicable required work remains unfinished, unverified, or blocked.

